#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153445 :tutorial/content "Suppose $a$ is an integer, and ${\\color{red}{n}}$ is a positive integer. As we've seen, the *division algorithm* states that there exists a unique integer $q$ and a unique positive integer ${\\color{blue}{r}}$ such that\n\n$$\na = q{\\color{red}{n}}+{\\color{blue}{r}}, \\qquad 0\\leq {\\color{blue}{r}} < {\\color{red}{n}}\n$$\n\nWhen two integers $a$ and $b$ have the *same remainder* ${\\color{blue}{r}}$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{n}}$, we say they are **congruent modulo** $\\boldsymbol n$, and we write\n$a\\equiv b\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\nThe number $n$ is called the **modulus**.\n\nTo demonstrate this concretely, consider the following list of numbers:\n\n$$\n1, \\qquad 6, \\qquad 11, \\qquad 16\n$$\n\nThese numbers are **congruent modulo** $\\boldsymbol 5$ because they each have the same remainder $({\\color{blue}{1}})$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{5}}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 0 \\cdot 5 + 1 \\\\\n6 &= 1 \\cdot 5 + 1 \\\\\n11 &= 2 \\cdot 5 + 1 \\\\\n16 &= 3 \\cdot 5 + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ≡6 & (mod5) \\\\ 1 & ≡11 & (mod5) \\\\ 1 & ≡16 & (mod5) \\\\ 6 & ≡11 & (mod5) \\\\ 6 & ≡16 & (mod5) \\\\ 11 & ≡16 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, consider the following list of numbers:\n\n$$\n2, \\qquad 7, \\qquad 12, \\qquad 17\n$$\n\nThese numbers are *also* congruent modulo $5$ because they each have a remainder of $\\color{blue}{2}$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 0 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\\\\n7 &= 1 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\\\\n12 &= 2 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\\\\n17 &= 3 \\cdot 5 + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ≡7 & (mod5) \\\\ 2 & ≡12 & (mod5) \\\\ 2 & ≡17 & (mod5) \\\\ 7 & ≡12 & (mod5) \\\\ 7 & ≡17 & (mod5) \\\\ 12 & ≡17 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo visualize congruence modulo $5$ more generally, let's write the sequence $0, \\: 1, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\ldots$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $5$ numbers.\n\n![](images/24/24c9af7d731f0be479f36367ce2e1deb0d2d691ffa575cfa843a1ca1742d2698.png)\n\nEach \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $5$. Every number on a given spoke is congruent modulo $5$ to every other number on that spoke." :tutorial/id #uuid "079c66bc-ad87-56ed-85da-3ad13ba65bd5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9010 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153449 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've been using the fact that $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $(a-b){:}$\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nTo prove this result, first note that if $a$ and $b$ have the same remainder $r$ when divided by $n$, then by the division algorithm\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= pn + r \\\\\nb &= qn + r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some integers $p$ and $q$.\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives\n\n$$\n(a-b) = (p-q)n\n$$\n\nNow, if we denote\n\n$$\nk=p-q\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n(a-b) = kn\n$$\n\nSo, $(a-b)$ is an integer multiple of $n$, which means that $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$.\n\nConversely, assume that $n \\mid (a-b)$. Then there is an integer $k$ such that $a-b= nk$.\n\nNow, by Euclid's division algorithm, there exist unique integers $m$ and $r\\in \\{0, 1, \\dots n-1\\}$ such that\n\n$$\nb={\\color{blue} n}m+\\color{red}r\n$$\n\nNote that this means that $b \\, \\cong \\, r \\, \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\, n)$. So, we have\n\n$$\na - b = nk \\mid ⟹a = b + nk; ⟹a = (nm + r) + nk; ⟹a = n(m + k) + r\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a \\, \\equiv \\, r \\, \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\, n)$ and $b \\, \\equiv \\, r \\, \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\, n)$, so $a \\, \\equiv \\, b \\, \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\, n)$.\n\nThis proves that if $n$ divides $(a-b)$ then $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $n$." :tutorial/id #uuid "5201f644-599c-5365-8b1d-acb9b2a8bc13" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9015 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Divisibility Property"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153471 :tutorial/content "In general, two integers $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $(a-b)$.\n$a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)$\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nFor example, the following numbers are congruent with respect to the given modulus:\n\n- $6\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$ because $5\\,\\mid\\, (6-1)$. There is no remainder:\n$(6-1)\\div 5 = 5\\div 5 = 1$\n- $-2\\equiv -8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$ because $6\\,\\mid\\, \\left((-2)-(-8)\\right)$. There is no remainder:\n$\\left((-2)-(-8)\\right)\\div 6 = 6\\div 6 = 1$\n\nHowever, the following numbers are not congruent with respect to the given modulus:\n\n- $6\\not\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$ because $4\\,\\not\\mid\\, (6-1)$. Note that $(6-1) = 5$, which is not divisible by $4$.\n- $0\\not\\equiv 13\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,12)$ because $12\\,\\not\\mid\\, (0-13)$. Note that $(0-13) = -13$, which is not divisible by $12$." :tutorial/id #uuid "30bcacad-ba7e-5d26-840a-8d0c84a74224" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9122 :tutorial/title "Determining Congruence Using Divisibility"}]}
